\text{det}(A) = \begin{vmatrix}
c_0 & c_1 & \cdots & c_n \\
c_1 & c_2 & \cdots & c_n \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
c_n & c_{n-1} & \cdots & c_1
\end{vmatrix}
\]

We use the property of determinants:
\[ 
\text{det}(A) = (-1)^{n+1} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k c_{n-k+1}
\]

**Step-by-step justification:**

1. **Summing Over All Indices:** The sum over all \( k \) from \( 0 \) to \( n-1 \):
   \[ 
   \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k c_{n-k+1} = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \cdot \left( c_{n-k+1} - c_k \right)
   \]
   
2. **Factorization:** Recognize that each term \( c_k c_{n-k+1} \) cancels out when multiplying by \( c_k \):
   \[ 
   \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k c_{n-k+1} = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \cdot \left( c_{n-k+1} - c_k \right) = \left( \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \right) \cdot \left( c_{n-k+1} - c_k \right)
   \]

3. **Combining Terms:** Multiply both sides by \( \frac{1}{c_k} \):
   \[ 
   \text{det}(A) = \frac{1}{c_k} \left( \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \cdot \left( c_{n-k+1} - c_k \right) \right)
   \]

Thus, the determinant of the matrix \( A \) is:
\[ 
\boxed{\frac{1}{c_k} \left( \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \cdot \left( c_{n-k+1} - c_k \right) \right)}
