To solve the problem, we need to determine the smallest integer \( n \) such that \( f(n) \) has no common divisors with \( p = 2 \):

\[ f(n) = (n-1)^7 - 14(n-1)^5 + 56(n-1)^3 - 56(n-1) + 22 \]

Substitute \( p = 2 \):
\[ f(2) = (2-1)^7 - 14(2-1)^5 + 56(2-1)^3 - 56(2-1) + 22 \]
\[ = 8 - 96 + 128 - 128 + 22 \]
\[ = 0 \]

Therefore, there exists exactly one integer \( n \) such that \( f(n) \) is not divisible by \( p = 2 \). Hence, the solution is:
\[ \boxed{0} \]<|im_end|>
